WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.667
Request a description. The reader asks for a public event description. The key identifies the resource and representation; private results need appropriate identity scoping.

2
00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:22.000
Serve a hit. A reusable cached value returns quickly. Its age matters: a fast response can still be too stale for the business operation.

3
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:32.083
Handle a miss. If the value is absent or expired, fetch from the source of truth. A miss is normal and must have bounded latency and safe error handling.

4
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:43.000
Control refill work. Many readers may miss the same hot key together. Coalesce loads or bound refill work so the cache does not amplify pressure on the database.

5
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:54.333
Store a temporary copy. Populate the cache with an explicit lifetime. Plan what happens after an update and whether serving an older value is acceptable during refresh.

6
00:00:54.333 --> 00:01:05.333
Test a cold cache. Remove the cache in a safe test. Observe source traffic, latency and errors. A warm hit-rate chart alone does not prove the architecture can recover.
